# 20 Generating and absorbing waves: relaxation and damping layers {#ch:zones}

A computational domain is finite, but many flows are not: waves come from
far away and leave, a wake leaves the box. The boundary conditions of
[chapter 18](18_boundary) are not enough on their own. A velocity inlet reflects every
wave that comes back to it, and a pressure outlet reflects unless the flow
reaching it is already calm. The usual remedy is to add layers inside the
domain, next to the boundaries, where the solution is gently pulled towards
a known state. Basilisk does not provide these as a module; they are a few
lines in user events, and this chapter derives them.

