## The strength profile

Both kinds of layer use a coefficient $\sigma(x)$ (units 1/s) that is zero
outside the layer and grows smoothly inside it. With $\tau\in[0,1]$ the
normalised distance into the layer, a good choice is
$$
\sigma(\tau) = \sigma_{\max}\,\tau^2(3 - 2\tau).
$$
This "smoothstep" starts from zero with zero slope, so a wave entering the
layer meets no sudden change; a sudden change in the medium reflects
waves, which is what the layer is meant to avoid.

## Damping layers: implicit Rayleigh damping {#sec:sponge}

A damping (sponge) layer adds a drag $-\sigma\mathbf{u}$ to the momentum
equation. Integrated explicitly, $u^{n+1} = u^n(1 - \sigma\Delta t)$ changes sign
and grows if $\sigma\Delta t> 2$. Integrated implicitly (backward Euler),
$$
\dfrac{u^{n+1} - u^n}{\Delta t} = -\sigma u^{n+1}
\;\Longrightarrow\;
u^{n+1} = \dfrac{u^n}{1 + \sigma\Delta t}
\;\Longleftrightarrow\;
\dfrac{u^{n+1} - u^n}{\Delta t} = -\dfrac{\sigma\,u^n}{1 + \sigma\Delta t},
$$
which is stable for any $\sigma\Delta t$. The last form is an acceleration that
depends only on known values, so it can be added to the face acceleration
$\mathbf{a}$ ([section 14.8](14_acceleration#sec:userforce)):

~~~literatec
static double sponge_sigma (double x) {
  double x0 = L0/2. - LAYER;                  // layer at the right end
  if (x <= x0) return 0.;
  double tau = clamp ((x - x0)/LAYER, 0., 1.);
  return SIGMA_MAX*sq(tau)*(3. - 2.*tau);
}

event acceleration (i++) {
  face vector av = a;
  foreach_face() {
    double s = sponge_sigma (x);
    if (s > 0. && fm.x[] > 0.)
      av.x[] -= s*uf.x[]/(1. + dt*s);
  }
}
~~~

A user `acceleration` event runs first in its slot ([chapter 2](02_language)),
so `uf` there is still the projected face velocity of the previous
step, a good estimate of $u^n$ on that face. Because the drag is put in
$\mathbf{a}$, on faces, it enters the projection like any other force: the pressure
adjusts to it and the face velocity remains divergence-free. Drag conserves
volume; it only removes momentum.

For a free surface, the outlet can also relax the volume fraction towards a
flat surface, $f_{\text{target}} = \text{clamp}(\tfrac12 - (y - h)/\Delta, 0, 1)$,
with the update of the next section. This removes the wave shape as well as
its velocity, but it is not volume-conserving.

## Relaxation zones: exact exponential relaxation

A relaxation zone pulls the solution towards a target state $q_T$ (an
incoming wave, a uniform flow):
$$
\dfrac{dq}{dt} = -\sigma\,(q - q_T).
$$
Over one step with $q_T$ held fixed, the exact solution is
$$
q^{n+1} = q_T + (q^n - q_T)\,e^{-\sigma\Delta t}
= q^n + w\,(q_T - q^n),\qquad w = 1 - e^{-\sigma\Delta t}.
$$
The weight $w$ is always between 0 and 1, so the update never overshoots,
whatever $\sigma\Delta t$. In a two-phase flow, relax $f$ and $\mathbf{u}$ together, in
the same cells and with the same weight, so that the momentum reconstructed
from them stays consistent:

~~~literatec
event relax (i++) {
  foreach() {
    double s = inlet_sigma (x);
    if (s > 0.) {
      double w = 1. - exp (-dt*s);
      double fT = target_fraction (x, y, t);     // e.g. exact cut-cell fraction of the wave
      f[]   += w*(fT - f[]);
      u.x[] += w*(fT*target_u (x, y, t) - u.x[]);
      u.y[] += w*(fT*target_v (x, y, t) - u.y[]);
    }
  }
}
~~~

This event has a new name, so it runs after the projection; the next
step's projection then corrects the face velocity built from the relaxed
cell velocities. Relaxing $f$ adds or removes fluid; that is how the zone
absorbs waves coming back to the inlet while holding the incident wave.

## Choosing the layers

The layer length is a physical constraint: for reflection of a few percent
the damping layer needs to be about one wavelength long. In a 2D test with a
0.75-wavelength layer, the reflection was about 4.5% with
$\sigma_{\max}$ equal to the wave frequency and grew to 7--17% with three
to eight times that value. A stronger $\sigma_{\max}$ does
not make up for a short layer: beyond some value, the drag itself acts as an
obstacle and reflects more. Keep the inlet zone short and away from the
region of interest, so it does not damp the scattered field you want to
measure. Then measure the reflection: in a cheap 2D run,
record the wave amplitude along the tank and compute the ratio
$(A_{\max} - A_{\min})/(A_{\max} + A_{\min})$ of the standing-wave envelope.

